From 8dff05a1fdff8785cf52eebe0bf8ad6fee4cf50e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 23:22:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09272/11362] Move the live ARC generator to Qwen3.8-27B and
 size the budget for its output

Operator directive 2026-08-16: prepare a live agent submission on Qwen3.8-27B
with settings for the 96 GB Blackwell card. Two of the three requested settings
are changed as asked; the third is not, because our own measurement on the
scored card refutes it. Details below.

CONTEXT BUDGET. Qwen3.8 is a reasoning model and writes far longer completions
than gemma-4-31B. Seven measured inductions ran 49,244 to 83,544 tokens, median
61,284. The kernel shipped CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_MAX_TOKENS=8192, which covers 0 of
those 7; a 64k budget covers only 4 of 7. A truncated completion is an unusable
engine, not a slow one. Raised to 131,072, which covers all seven with margin
for the tail we have not sampled and stays inside the model's own trained
context (qwen35.context_length = 262144). The pool derives from the budget, so
this resolves to n_ctx 614,400 at 4 slots, 153,600 per slot, a predicted 47,965
MiB against the 97,250 MiB the card reports free. max_tokens is a cap, so unused
headroom costs pool VRAM and never wall clock. The induce timeout moves 1200s to
2400s for the same reason: the worst measured Qwen3.8 induction is ~1053s on
this card, and a timeout that fires degrades the agent to LLM-off silently.

SLOT COUNT UNCHANGED AT 4, contrary to the request. A prior kernel run already
measured this on the real card, at a realistic 21,879-token prompt: aggregate
throughput FALLS as slots rise, 27.4 tok/s at K=1, 18.1 at K=4, 14.2 at K=8.
More slots helps only at small prompts, and ARC prompts are not small. K=8
against the shipped pool also returned HTTP 400 "request (21880 tokens) exceeds
the available context size (13312 tokens)" -- the divided-context branch.

WHAT THE SWAP COSTS, recorded so it is not rediscovered. No MTP draft head ships
for Qwen3.8-27B, so speculative decoding turns off: roughly 1.8x slower decode
than gemma. Combined with ~9x longer completions that is about 17x the wall
clock per induction, roughly 49% of the 12h eval against gemma's 3%. It is
bought with better induction quality on the three games measured head to head so
far, which is thin evidence.

GUARDS. Five submission guards failed on the first pass and all five are now
green. Two were ALREADY FAILING on HEAD, verified against a clean worktree:
REQUIRED_DATASETS named carnot-gemma4-31b-it-gguf plus a separate mtp-head
dataset while kernel-metadata.json requested the single combined
carnot-gemma4-31b-it-qat-gguf, so the readiness gate was reporting the package
blocked on datasets that were never going to appear. That is exactly the drift
test_exp4756_required_datasets_match_what_the_kernel_actually_attaches exists to
catch, and it was live.

One of the three new failures was a real bug the guard caught: pinning the GGUF
head filter to a model-specific string meant a leftover gemma draft head was no
longer excluded from main-model candidates and could be bound as the generator,
which loads, serves, and answers nonsense. The filter is now the generic "mtp-"
prefix, which excludes any draft head regardless of which model it drafts for.

test_no_submission_gate_still_names_a_retired_generator_dataset asserted
"qwen" not in slug, which was correct only while gemma was pinned and would
forbid the very generator being shipped. It now derives the retired families
from the live pin, so the next swap needs no edit there.

OPERATOR ACTION REQUIRED. The kernel attaches the model as a Kaggle dataset and
iancblenke/carnot-qwen38-27b-gguf (~16 GB) DOES NOT EXIST. Only the operator can
create it, and the kernel push fails at dataset resolution until it does.
kaggle_dataset_uploaded is False and asserted False, which is the same mechanism
that tracked the gemma upload before 2026-07-28. Flip it to True in the commit
that records the upload. Submission itself remains operator-only.
